# Task Lifecycle

When generating task management commands, incorporate these patterns.

## Core Types

TaskStatus = pending | in_progress | completed | blocked | cancelled

Epic {
  name
  status        // 📋 PLANNED | 🚧 IN PROGRESS | ✅ COMPLETED
  goal          // One sentence
  overview      // Starts with WHY
  tasks[]       // Each has description + Given/Should requirements
}

FileLocations {
  epics => tasks/[epic-name].md
  archive => tasks/archive/YYYY-MM-DD-[epic-name].md
  plan => plan.md
  log => activity-log.md
}

## Task Planning

planTask() {
  gatherContext |> decompose |> sequence |> defineSuccess |> createEpic |> awaitApproval
}

PlanningRules {
  Atomic tasks, ~50 lines each, independently testable
  Order by dependencies, identify approval gates
  Given/Should requirements per task
}

## Task Execution

executeTask() {
  loadEpic |> identifyNext |> executeSingle |> validate |> report |> awaitApproval
}

(multiple tasks in_progress) => invalid, only one at a time

## Epic Completion

completeEpic() {
  Update status to COMPLETED with date
  Move to archive with date prefix
  Update plan.md
}

## Activity Logging

LogCategories {
  🚀 feature | 🐛 fix | 🔄 refactor | 📝 docs | 🎨 ui | 🔒 security
}

WhatToLog {
  Log => epic completions, major features, architecture decisions
  Skip => config, file org, minor fixes, dependencies
}

## Commands to Generate

Commands {
  /task => create epic, decompose, await approval
  /execute => run next task, single-task only
  /plan => review plan.md, suggest next steps
  /log => log completion to activity-log.md
  /status => show epic and task state
}

## Lifecycle Calibration

| Aspect | PoC | MVP | Production |
|--------|-----|-----|------------|
| Epic formality | Informal | Structured | Full |
| Task size | Coarse | ~50 lines | ~50 lines |
| Approval gates | Optional | Per task | Per task |
| Plan tracking | Optional | plan.md | + archive |

Constraints {
  Single-task execution only.
  Explicit approval between tasks.
  Epic overview starts with WHY.
  Requirements use Given/Should format.
  No task numbering (names only).
  Keep epic files minimal.
}
